# Prospective selector module control measurement

This ignored stage prepares a future comparison of the unchanged production reducer, the unchanged two-module selector candidate, and the authenticated released Grafana reducer. No timing or memory measurement has run in this stage. Source layout, dispatch, and engine inlining are hypotheses. Neither correctness evidence nor a future median establishes general superiority or authorizes integration.

## Sources and authority

The baseline is production `native-calculations.mjs`, SHA-256 `9269cf8c32be9bf2c075b67019e82e41272d9495f0321fefc17b2c5695a91371`. The prospective entry is `candidate-module-control.mjs`, SHA-256 `33574a99fd21029a7b564b1d9316c2b6d7389641c6b607d6bea61dc022457dd3`; its static helper `selector-control.mjs` is separately bound as `a6622d09853a8c1b550e6de919d0908a3647660bde561f250fcee9a81864763a`. Both are required, preserved, and checked before and after child execution. One selected implementation can consist of two native modules.

The independent original is the actual released `@grafana/data@13.2.3` CJS reducer, SHA-256 `36f6f81fc3ccc8708f8f42a306a0123b717d46320aa888df5acd0e0673514443`, using the actually resolved `lodash@4.17.23` source, SHA-256 `71b4b7f5d2fc2d69399fd3edd7104fa10f9fd73fc600130825c3032ee8c50dad`. Authentication checks the exact registry archives, registry integrity, retained official source bytes, archive members, and resolved dependency. Exact six loaded original CJS files and both archives are preserved in `preserved/original/`. Node is v24.16.0, x64 Windows, executable SHA-256 `b3094d0b49f9ad602262a9921551737bb97637c05dd357a06ae98188d7290aa3`.

`results/source-binding-audit.json` proves that the standard body, complete shared special body, multi-selection/cache tail, and transport tail remain byte-identical to baseline. Removing the entry import and reinserting the helper exactly reconstructs the previous inline candidate. The older selector receipt is preserved byte-for-byte as `preserved/selector-validation.json`, SHA-256 `b582c0da35dcd8b81a701cd1252c995c0ffb1e7307932b5b9f1279e737378aa6`. Its historical 2,910,751 reducer comparisons include 48,240 cache comparisons; it also binds 14,240 separate helper comparisons, held-out/long controls, 9,984 new controls, and 42 adversarial selections. These are historical results for the exact same entry and helper bytes. This stage has not rerun the full corpus. It authenticates selected prior inventory entries and exact final receipts; it does not claim a new read of every older 68/1,069 inventory entry.

The original computes expectations separately in `prepare-manifest.mjs`. Each final fixture records exact actual input JSON, canonical input digest, and canonical fresh, full cached reread, and cached subset packets. Canonical packets preserve undefined, sparse slots, IEEE special values, object references, and result/cache aliases. The initial and final seven-case preflights together made 14 fresh original calculations and 28 cached reads. They ran no warmup, transient, retention, memory snapshot, or timing loops.

## Fixed workloads

The plan has seven workloads, seven repeats, and three roles: 147 sequential fresh children for timing and another 147 for isolated memory. Roles rotate by repeat. Every field has ordinary array values, numeric type, connected null mode, empty ordinary state, and the synthetic name. No measured result is used to change these inputs, repetitions, limits, candidate, or acceptance gates.

| Workload | Samples | Selection | Timing calls / warmup | Memory transient / retained |
|---|---:|---|---:|---:|
| smallStandard | 100 | mean, min, max, sum, delta | 100,000 / 20,000 | 100,000 / 10,000 |
| largeStandard | 25,000 | same five | 2,000 / 500 | 2,000 / 10,000 |
| singlePercentile | 25,000 | p95 | 200 / 100 | 200 / 256 |
| allPercentiles | 25,000 | all 99 original percentile IDs | 3 / 2 | 16 / 16 |
| allCalculations | 25,000 | all 124 original built-in IDs | 3 / 2 | 16 / 16 |
| constructedPercentile | 25,000 | p95 | 200 / 100 | 200 / 256 |
| adversarialFallbackPercentile | 25,000 | p95 | 200 / 100 | 200 / 256 |

The first five inputs use the previous deterministic pattern `i % 23 === 0 ? null : ((i * 7879) % 7919) - 3959.25`. Their logical inputs, selections, fresh output/cache packets, warmup counts, transient counts, and retention counts match the previous comparison, as checked against the preserved old manifest. Timing call counts also retain the prior five specifications.

`constructedPercentile` uses the new formula and xorshift32 seed `0x41d7be69` fixed in `workloads.mjs`: null every 41st sample; otherwise `((i*i*17)%104729)*0.00390625 + (random()-0.5)*0.125 + floor(i/701)*3.25`. The seed is reused from earlier selector controls; the generator is a distinct constructed workload, not an independence claim about every previous input. Its canonical input SHA-256 is `f6a28dee5654eefae476183308950ded0ec4055a2d6bc8354a35dc67e36e79cb`.

`adversarialFallbackPercentile` uses organ-pipe values `i < n/2 ? i+0.125 : n-i+0.125`, canonical input SHA-256 `9ef1e5ba742b6e4c96311e072bd3b65faaed1a4ae27394ca52a25d9e4b65b299`. The repeated six-shape, seven-percentile adversarial corpus makes 42 original selections and 84 exact baseline/candidate comparisons. Precise inspector code coverage confirms six fallback hits, including this organ-pipe/p95 case. Coverage is a control-flow check, not a performance profile. This 42-case corpus deliberately reuses the accepted adversarial shapes rather than claiming new independent shapes.

The initial prospective organ-pipe/p1 choice did not reach fallback. Its outputs were exact across all 84 implementation comparisons, but its fallback assertion failed with exit 1. That attempt's exact source, initial manifest, result, and failure diagnosis are preserved in `failures/organ-pipe-p1-hypothesis/`; the initial fixture files and manifest remain separate. The initial result file's `status: passed` described completed output checks before the assertion and must not be used as acceptance of its fallback claim. The full initial console stack was not captured to a raw file; the preserved failure diagnosis states this limitation. Selection changed to p95 during correctness preparation, before any measurement. No failed attempt or measurement is discarded.

## Correctness and host readiness

`results/harness-readiness.json` binds sixteen sources before and after four harness test groups, three memory check-only children with 21 fresh/cache packet checks each, and the timing check-only child with 63 checks across all three implementations. The test groups cover IEEE/sparse/reference serialization, both-module import binding, 15 stale/invalid/future authorization rejections across five entrypoints, and PowerShell observer parsing. Check-only children run no measured loops, forced-GC checkpoints, observer process, or memory snapshots. New measurement branches have not yet executed; branch-specific runtime failures remain possible and must be retained.

`results/private-host-routing.json` binds a private product-shaped fixture assembled from exact production host, catalog, storage, Metrics client/core/server snapshots. Actual loopback HTTP checks client/core serving, three private module URLs returning 404, the 124-calculation catalog, and a p95 reduction/cache packet through the prospective backend. The fixture host closed after its checks. No existing user host, browser, credentials, user store, app source, results, catalog, or production metadata was changed.

The backend server imports the reducer; the browser client invokes its API. The existing host allowlist serves client/core/markdown modules, and intentionally does not serve the server, native reducer, or helper. An adjacent backend-only helper needs no browser static-route change. A future browser import would require a fresh explicit routing review. The copied fixture remains local under `.local/private-host-fixture/`; it contains generated copies/owned temporary directories, is excluded from the frozen stage inventory, and its executed file identities are bound in the host receipt and preserved source snapshots.

## Timing and memory boundaries

Timing uses sequential fresh children and rotates the role order. Each child prevalidates all three reducers, warms only its selected role, then measures fresh-field reduction calls. Last-field packet/cache and unchanged-input checks run after the timer. All three implementations are loaded in timing workers; their memory fields are diagnostic and never described as isolated memory. Each timing child has a 60-second deadline and 2 MiB output cap. The outer timing controller has a 600-second deadline and 4 MiB output cap. Raw child stdout/stderr, failures, all ranges, original/baseline comparisons, and regressions are retained. An outer controller timeout can terminate its direct process while an active timing grandchild continues until its own 60-second timeout; this harness does not claim Windows Job Object/process-tree isolation. These are trusted read-only local reducer workers, not an arbitrary subprocess sandbox.

Isolated memory reuses the preserved prior tested worker/observer lifecycle and caps. Each fresh child loads exactly one reducer implementation: original and its six CJS files, baseline, or the two native candidate files. The parent authenticates sources/expectations before children. Native child CJS cache must be empty. The Windows owned-PID observer must report a matching PID before sending the start handshake. Children record initialization, shared input, warmed baseline, transient block tails, transient completion, retention after GC, and released after GC. Three GC calls with event-loop yields precede settled checkpoints. The limits remain 256 MiB old-space, 512 MiB observed RSS, 120 seconds per child, 4 MiB child/observer stdout and 64 KiB observer stderr, with observer polling every 100 ms. RSS checkpoints and polling can miss between-sample peaks; Node old-space excludes other memory. Output buffers can exceed a cap by the arriving chunk before termination. Direct-process exit/PID observation is not atomic process-tree ownership or protection against arbitrary PID reuse. The observer is stopped and awaited after each owned child. There is no separate aggregate memory-run deadline; the conservative all-children timeout envelope is about 4.9 hours plus startup/cleanup, not the expected duration below.

After the retained snapshot, each retained field is serialized and checked fresh, with a full cached read and a cached subset read. These serializer objects are released before the release-only GC checkpoint, but their common allocator/heap-capacity/RSS pressure can persist. Release observations are diagnostics, not proof of a leak or pure allocation cost. This is more validation pressure than the older single-packet retained-field validation. Likewise the new timing worker tracks `lastField` and verifies its packet outside the timer. These are common protocols across the three roles within this prospective study; byte-identical benchmark scaffolding or numerical equivalence across earlier studies is not claimed.

No cumulative allocation counter, exact physical peak measurement, universal engine behavior, or machine-level isolation is promised. Root and agents must explicitly pause tests, builds, browser actions, publication, and source scans during each future coordinated interval. Unrelated Windows services/background activity remain uncontrolled. Correctness/source authentication and harness startup are disclosed separately from timed call loops. A coordinator timestamp is an operational authorization guard for trusted local callers; it is not cryptographic proof of a human authorization message.

## Future execution and freeze

After current combined tests and publication are complete, request a fresh root-coordinated interval for timing, then a separate explicit fresh timestamp for isolated memory. Invoke the stage's `timing-runner.mjs --quiet-confirmed <new ISO UTC timestamp>` and `memory-runner.mjs --quiet-confirmed <new ISO UTC timestamp> control`. Timestamps must be newer than this preflight, valid UTC, and not in the future. Never manufacture an authorization timestamp. Authenticate the final readiness inventory and current source hashes before execution. Keep all old studies, candidate files, manifests, thresholds, and production frozen; write only new attempt outputs. Report completion/branch failure promptly and await explicit close before consolidation or heavy checks.

Reserve **3–5 minutes for timing and 10–15 minutes for isolated memory**, with room to extend or stop if a declared limit fails. These are conservative prospective scheduling estimates, not measured durations or success guarantees. Prior 105-child memory sequences took 188.35 and 181.88 seconds and exceeded their provisional 2–3 minute estimate. This plan has 147 children and extra cache/serializer validation. Its full hard timeout envelopes exceed these expected reservations. Retain actual durations against estimates and any killed/partial/limit-hit children.

`readiness-validation.json` will inventory the exact stage sources, proofs, fixtures, failures, and source snapshots before a future measurement. Archives are inventoried locally but excluded from any future public source-only preservation: `preserved/original/release.tgz` and `preserved/original/lodash-4.17.23.tgz`; exact hashes and sizes are recorded. No native executable is copied into this stage. Production and older selected artifacts are verified unchanged. No timing/memory superiority, exhaustive feature enumeration, universal correctness, full product completion, or production selection is established; all such gates remain false.
